# Deliberate exceptions to scripts/check-vacuous-assertions.sh.
#
# Format, one per line:
#
#   RULE | path-relative-to-repo-root | anchor text | reason
#
# The anchor is a text snippet from the offending line (or, for the bats body
# rules VAC003/VAC005/VAC006, from the `@test "..."` header). It is text rather
# than a line number on purpose: a line number rots on the next edit anywhere
# above it, which trains people to renumber the allowlist without rereading the
# entry. Text anchoring fails when the line is genuinely gone and survives when
# it merely moved.
#
# Three things make an entry FAIL THE BUILD, all of them exit 1:
#
#   * the path no longer exists
#   * the anchor text is no longer in that file
#   * the entry suppresses nothing
#
# The last is the sharpest. An exception for a finding that no longer occurs
# reads as "reviewed and accepted" while describing nothing, and it silently
# pre-approves whatever reoccupies that name later. If the code it excused has
# been fixed, delete the line — that is the whole contract.
#
# An entry is a written-down decision. If you cannot state the reason in a
# clause, the finding is not an exception; it is a defect you have not fixed yet.
#
# ── Entries ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every finding from VAC001-VAC006's first run over this tree was a true
# positive and was fixed, not excused:
#
#   test/audit-anchor-keys-path.bats     grep "must not match" tests that passed
#   test/ci-installer-privileges.bats    on a missing file (grep exit 2 reads as
#   test/ci-lock.bats                    "pattern absent" under `-ne 0`)
#   test/supercred-image-visibility.bats a fail-closed test that `bash $shim`
#                                        would have satisfied with exit 127
#   d2-osramp-apps/test/osramp.bats      two `run verify` lines and no status
#                                        check: green whether NATS was up, down,
#                                        or the cluster unreachable
#
# VAC007 found four. Two were real and were fixed
# (scripts/check-deployed-staleness.sh, test/terraform-environment-keys.bats).
# The two below are the same shape in a PRODUCER: a function whose whole job is
# to emit a list on stdout, where empty is a legitimate answer rather than a
# swallowed failure, and where the caller — not the producer — is the place that
# has to refuse. In both cases every caller already does, at the line named. The
# rule cannot see across a call frame, so this is the honest use of an
# exception rather than a rule tightened until it fits.
#
# Both entries are anchored on the mapfile line, so they go stale the moment
# either function is restructured, which is exactly when the reasoning above
# needs rereading.

VAC007 | scripts/lib/spire-trust-anchor.sh | mapfile -t encoded_pems | producer: openbao_jwt_validation_keys emits OpenBao's validation keys, and holding none is a real cluster state rather than a discarded error. Its only non-test caller, assert_openbao_accepts_spire_jwt_keys, refuses loudly on empty at the `[[ -n "$bao_out" ]]` guard ("OpenBao holds NO JWT validation keys at all") before comparing anything. Guarding here as well would turn a reportable state into a library-level abort.
VAC007 | test/helpers/toolchain-contract.bash | mapfile -t names | producer: contract_remote_path_vars emits name/variable pairs for callers to assert over. Both callers in test/terraform-env-contract.bats already refuse on empty — `[ -n "$output" ]` at line 426 and `[ -n "$pairs" ] || { echo "no exported name/variable pairs found"; return 1; }` at line 483 — so the emptiness check exists, one frame up, in the tests that would otherwise go vacuous.

# VAC008's own comment states it ships with no allowlist entries because every
# prior finding was a true positive. These four are the first exception, and
# they are a detector limitation rather than a defect: scan_pipefail_early_exit_grep
# tracks quote state per LINE (`U[i] = unquote(L[i])`), not across lines, so it
# cannot see that build.sh:79-105 is one multi-line single-quoted string handed
# to `bash -c` inside a docker container — exactly false-positive class 1 named
# in the rule's own comment ("a pipeline quoted as data"), just spanning more
# lines than the per-line tracker follows. The outer script's `set -euo pipefail`
# (build.sh:22) is attributed to these four `grep -q` lines because the scanner
# reads them as the outer script's own text, but they never run under it: the
# embedded script opens its own `bash -c` process with only `set -e` (no
# pipefail) at its own top, confirmed by reading the full quoted body — there is
# no `pipefail` anywhere inside the single-quoted span. Without pipefail, grep's
# own exit status (0 match / 1 no-match) is the pipeline's status regardless of
# the producer's SIGPIPE, so the SIGPIPE-inverts-the-verdict mechanism this rule
# exists to catch cannot occur in the context where these lines actually execute.
VAC008 | d2-signalloom/apps/stun-sentinel/bpf/build.sh | grep -q "EM_BPF" | inside the docker-run bash -c string (build.sh:79-105), which sets only `set -e`, no pipefail — confirmed by reading the whole single-quoted body. The scanner attributes the outer script's `set -euo pipefail` to this line because its quote-state tracking is per-line and cannot see a multi-line single-quoted span; see the note above this block.
VAC008 | d2-signalloom/apps/stun-sentinel/bpf/build.sh | \.BTF | same nested bash -c as the EM_BPF entry above, no pipefail in effect where either of these two lines actually runs. One short anchor covers both the ".BTF" and ".BTF.ext" findings: apply_allowlist's `awk -v A=` unescapes `\.` to a plain `.` (with a warning), so what it matches against is the bare substring ".BTF" -- present in both lines' own text, verified directly against both. A longer anchor including the surrounding `grep -q "` text does NOT work here: grep -qF (the file-presence half of this check) reads the anchor unescaped straight from this line, so the literal backslash it needs to find the real file's `"\.BTF"` sits at a DIFFERENT character offset than the awk-mangled version needs for the index() match, and no single anchor string satisfies both checks when it spans across the backslash. Anchoring on the backslash-dot fragment alone sidesteps the conflict entirely.
VAC008 | d2-signalloom/apps/stun-sentinel/bpf/build.sh | grep -q "$sec" | same nested bash -c as the EM_BPF entry above, no pipefail in effect where this line actually runs.
